WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.410
Horror is a genre that requires exact timing
and expert pacing in order to deliver the

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:07.240
maximum amount of scares.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:11.111
While every horror fan has a well-curated
list of their favorite horror movies, there

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:14.510
are lots of incredible horror shorts you can
watch for free online.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:19.770
Short, sweet, and terrifying, here are some
of the best spooky short films.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.490
The Ten Steps

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:25.460
Children left alone by their parents is common
in horror, but it's effective for a reason

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.430
— parents usually represent safety.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:33.340
The Ten Steps is a perfect example of a short,
atmospheric horror film with a grim punchline.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.289
After the power goes out while Katie is babysitting
her younger brother, her father talks her

00:00:37.289 --> 00:00:41.609
through it over the phone — but by the end
she ends up in a very different place than

00:00:41.609 --> 00:00:43.329
she imagined.

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:44.760
Downstairs

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.559
One of the best horror shorts of the last
few years, Downstairs follows a security guard

00:00:49.559 --> 00:00:52.250
investigating mysterious sounds in the basement.

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:57.340
The film sticks to the basics of horror movie
rules: an early warning of things to come,

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:01.179
some light character work to establish who's
going to meet the monsters, and then just

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.100
minute after minute of terror.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.480
"Alright, that's f---ed up."

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:09.820
Symmetropia

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.020
There can something a little unsettling about
perfect symmetry.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:17.920
Perhaps it's because things tend to be naturally
chaotic and we expect disorder, but for whatever

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.050
the reason, Symmetropia taps into something
very creepy.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:27.000
For anyone who's ever been creeped out by
a Wes Anderson movie, Symmetropia leads you

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.479
to some very unusual places, and shows that
the strange fear of perfectly balanced things

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:35.360
might not be so irrational after all.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.510
"Yo, banana boy!"

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:40.909
The Facts in the Case of Mister Hollow

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:45.040
One of the most inventive horror shorts in
recent memory, The Facts in the Case of Mister

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.329
Hollow continually plays on your expectations
and changes of perspective.

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:54.869
The film shows an old photograph depicting
what seems at first like a happy family vacation.

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:59.890
As the camera dances around the photo, details
leap out at the viewer, and the final shot

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.770
paints a very different picture than the opening
scene.

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:04.840
3 Versos

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:09.619
A strong production design can often carry
horror movies with weaker scripts, but 3 Versos

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:10.920
doesn't need the help.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.680
The script is surprisingly dense, layering
a deeper mythology and some hidden surprises

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:17.270
into a naturalistic dialogue.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:23.100
3 Versos is clearly indebted to classic black
and white horror films of the early 20th century,

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:27.230
but with a multitude of visual influences
that distort the viewer's perspective about

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:31.730
when the short takes place — adding to the
discomfort of watching it.

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:33.250
Alexia

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:38.330
Argentinian film Alexia acknowledges what
we've always known — that Facebook is evil

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:39.739
and trying to kill us.

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:41.269
Well, not quite.

00:02:41.269 --> 00:02:46.360
The gradual build-up of guilt and subtle implications
of abuse in a relationship help take this

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:52.299
short to the next level, while the set design
and escalating discomfort keep it quite scary.

00:02:52.299 --> 00:02:53.720
Don't Move

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:58.620
Before the opening title, Don't Move has already
gotten all of its backstory out of the way,

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:03.260
as six friends accidentally summon a demon
that's going to kill five of them, and let

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:04.290
one live.

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:08.760
The real treat of the short is the incredible
creature design and special effects.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:13.319
Don't Move was supposedly created on a low
budget, but you'll be hard pressed to notice

00:03:13.319 --> 00:03:15.060
as you watch it.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.450
The Birch

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:21.650
It's not hard to see the similarities between
Don't Move and The Birch, as they share filmmakers.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:25.500
Both shorts have an incredible creature design
and a stripped down story.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:30.319
The Birch follows a bullied young boy given
a mysterious book and a promise from his grandmother

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:32.349
that someone will protect him.

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:35.941
Sometimes it's hard to see the forest for
the tree monsters, but let's just say, when

00:03:35.941 --> 00:03:39.310
the tree monsters look this good, that's not
a bad thing.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:41.459
This House Has People In It

00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:46.420
A series of unexplained edits from home security
camera footage is the setup of the short film

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.590
This House Has People In It — which ends
up being a surrealist nightmare.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:54.930
Part of Adult Swim's "Infomercials" segments
which air at 4 a.m., this short taps into

00:03:54.930 --> 00:04:00.120
a deeply uncomfortable sense of voyeurism,
like watching a reality show from a horrific

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.920
alternate reality where every day is terrible.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:04.939
Lights Out

00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:09.540
With a very simple concept and execution,
Lights Out is about a woman who sees a frightening

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.000
figure that appears whenever the lights turn
off.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.350
Like comedy, horror can depend on a punchline
that a viewer wasn't expecting, and this short

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.690
is the perfect example.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.620
The Smiling Man

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:24.560
Most horror movies hide the monster from view
for as long as possible to keep the viewer

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:30.530
in suspense, but The Smiling Man gives you
an upsettingly long, direct look at the monster.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:35.030
The short focuses on a little girl who follows
a trail of balloons into her kitchen where

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.550
a smiling man starts to play with her.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:41.310
Which is obviously very very bad news.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:42.830
Skypemare

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:47.419
In Skypemare, a routine Skype call between
friends goes horribly wrong when it looks

00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:50.190
like someone is moving in the room behind
one of them.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:54.830
Following in the storied tradition of technology
being a delivery system for horror, the film's

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:58.940
real villain is the short's most unexpected
twist.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.190
On My Way

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:04.710
Hollywood loves the one-take tracking shot,
but we've never seen a tracking shot for a

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.270
horror short quite like the one in On My Way.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:11.720
A man drives through town on his way to a
party, while something strange has happened

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:13.620
to the residents of the town.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:18.190
If nothing else, hopefully watching the short
will encourage you to actually arrive at parties

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:19.570
when you say you will.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:20.800
Thanks for watching!

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:23.310
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:25.530
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

